58:/// The two tools whose permissions this phase exists to pin, per agent.
61:/// Every permission key the reviewer must deny, and why each one is named.
63:/// The list is opencode's own key set. The schema types `AgentConfig.permission`
64:/// with `read`, `edit`, `glob`, `grep`, `list`, `bash`, `task`,
65:/// `external_directory`, `todowrite`, `question`, `webfetch`, `websearch`,
66:/// `lsp`, `doom_loop` and `skill`; every one of them that is not a read-only
67:/// tool is here.
69:/// - `edit` covers `write`, `edit` and `apply_patch` — one key, not three.
70:/// - `bash` is the shell the reviewer has no business running.
71:/// - `task` is what stops the reviewer from delegating to a subagent, and the
72:///   built-in `general` subagent has full tool access.
73:/// - `webfetch` and `websearch` are the two ways out of the checkout: one is
74:///   granted by the global config, the other by opencode's permissive default.
75:/// - `skill` loads a skill file into the reviewer's context. A skill is
76:///   instructions the reviewer then follows, and it can be fetched from disk
77:///   or from a URL — a grant, not a read.
78:/// - `lsp` runs language-server queries. The reviewer has no use for it and no
79:///   reason to hold it.
80:/// - `question` asks a human. A pipeline review has no human to ask, and the
81:///   answer would be the only way to widen the reviewer.
82:/// - `todowrite` writes to the session, not to the tree — but it is still a
83:///   grant, and the reviewer is granted nothing it does not need.
84:/// - `doom_loop` is opencode's repeated-call guard. It defaults to `ask`, and
85:///   the pipeline runs `opencode run` with nobody to answer a prompt, so it is
86:///   denied outright: the guard blocks the repeated call instead of waiting
87:///   for an answer that will never come.
88:/// - `external_directory` is the one the global config grants as
89:///   `{"**": "allow"}`.
91:/// opencode's defaults are permissive ("Most permissions default to `allow`"),
92:/// so a key that is not declared is a key that is allowed.
107:/// The permission keys the reviewer may keep: the read-only tools its job
108:/// needs, and nothing else.
110:/// The shipped file does not declare them. opencode already allows `read`,
111:/// `glob` and `grep` by default, and declaring `read: "allow"` would be an
112:/// *widening* rather than a statement of intent: opencode's default `read`
113:/// rules deny `.env` files, and an agent-level `read: "allow"` is appended
114:/// after them, so it would win the last-match and re-allow them.
117:/// The permission keys whose schema type is the bare action only.
119:/// opencode types the rest of the keys as `PermissionRuleConfig`, which is
120:/// either a bare action or an object of pattern → action. These five are typed
121:/// as the action alone, so an object of patterns is not a value opencode can
122:/// apply to them.
131:/// The mode an agent must declare to be selectable as `--agent <name>`.
134:/// The agents this file is allowed to declare.
136:/// The project config is the pipeline's agent file: it declares the reviewer
137:/// and the verifier, and nothing else. A third name is the hazard — `build`
138:/// above all, because `rbops` runs every phase's implementer as
139:/// `--agent build`. opencode resolves a name in the project config first and
140:/// only then in the global one, so a `build` block here would OVERRIDE the
141:/// global config for that name: the implementer's permissions would move
142:/// without the global file changing, and without any other gate noticing.
145:/// The project config filename opencode looks for at the working directory.
