252:/// Require an agent to declare `mode: "primary"`.
254:/// `--agent <name>` names a TOP-LEVEL agent. opencode resolves the name and,
255:/// when the agent it finds is a subagent, prints `agent "reviewer" is a
256:/// subagent, not a primary agent. Falling back to default agent` and runs the
257:/// default agent instead — which here is `build`, whose `edit` and `bash` are
258:/// `allow`. The permission block would then be read and never applied, so the
259:/// mode is as load-bearing as the permissions it guards.
260:fn check_mode(agents: &Map<String, Value>, name: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
282:/// Reject a grant to the read-only agent for any tool outside the allowed set.
284:/// The reviewer is read-only in full, so the permission block is an
285:/// ALLOW-LIST: [`REVIEWER_ALLOWED`] may be allowed and every other key must
286:/// deny. A third key granted beside the pinned ones — `websearch`, `skill`,
287:/// `lsp`, `todowrite` — is a reviewer that can act, and
288:/// [`check_agent`] looks up exactly [`TOOLS`], so it would never visit them.
289:/// The list is what makes the rule checkable in the other direction too: a key
290:/// that is not on it cannot be granted, whether it is one opencode documents
291:/// today or one it adds tomorrow.
292:fn check_no_grants_beyond(agents: &Map<String, Value>, name: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
318:/// Require every key in [`REVIEWER_DENIED`] to be present AND to deny.
320:/// This is the check the other two cannot make. [`check_agent`] looks up
321:/// exactly [`TOOLS`], and [`check_no_grants_beyond`] visits only the keys that
